   form="Novel in monthly parts (20 numbers, 1852-53)",
   desc="Published in twenty monthly parts from March 1852 to September 1853, the first of the great 'dark' novels, and the most technically ambitious: two narrators alternate, Esther Summerson in the first person and an unnamed third-person narrator in the present tense, and the Court of Chancery presides over both.",
   plot="Jarndyce and Jarndyce, a Chancery suit over a contested will, has consumed generations of litigants; its costs finally consume the whole estate at the close. Esther Summerson, raised by the harsh Mrs Rachael and sent for by John Jarndyce as companion to his young cousins Richard Carstone and Ada Clare, discovers in the course of the novel that she is the illegitimate daughter of Lady Dedlock, whose secret the lawyer Tulkinghorn discovers and is murdered for; the murder is solved by Inspector Bucket, the first police detective in English fiction. Lady Dedlock dies at the pauper graveyard where her lover is buried, Richard dies of the Chancery suit, and Esther marries Woodcourt.",
