   sig="Great Expectations is the most perfectly constructed of Dickens's novels and the central English novel about class and shame; its treatment of Pip's gentlemanly self-invention and of Magwitch's colonial fortune made it a foundational text for later postcolonial and class criticism, and its two endings are a permanent case study in narrative authority.",
