EMILY = wks("Emily Brontë", [
 dict(title="Wuthering Heights", year="1847",
   gb="Sensation Novel, Novel of Development and the Growth Story", tb="The American wilderness and the myth of the forest, Art versus life, and the romance of the artist, Self-destruction, melancholy, and the coroner's verdict, Madness, the asylum, and the diagnosis of the will".replace("The American wilderness and the myth of the forest", "Regional identity and the American sectional character"),
   mb="Sensation Fiction and the Serial Plot, Pre-Raphaelitism and the Truth to Nature", comp=["1845", "1846"],
   form="Novel in three volumes (with Agnes Grey), framed narrative",
   desc="Published in December 1847 in three volumes together with Anne Brontë's Agnes Grey, under the pseudonym Ellis Bell, and the one Victorian novel that has never been domesticated into the conventions of its decade: a chronicle of two families on the Yorkshire moors, told by two partial narrators, in which the passion between Catherine Earnshaw and Heathcliff exceeds every social and religious form available to it.",
   plot="Mr Lockwood, the tenant of Thrushcross Grange, visits his landlord Heathcliff at Wuthering Heights and is told the history of the house by the servant Nelly Dean. The foundling Heathcliff, brought home from Liverpool by Mr Earnshaw, and Catherine Earnshaw grow up inseparable on the moors; Catherine marries Edgar Linton for social position, Heathcliff returns wealthy and embittered, and the second half of the novel follows his revenge on the next generation — Hindley's son Hareton, and Catherine's daughter Cathy, whom he forces to marry his dying son Linton — until his own death releases the pair into their marriage and the ghosts of the first Catherine and Heathcliff are reported on the moor.",
   context="The West Riding of Yorkshire in the 1770s-1802, the yeoman and gentry economy of the moorland farms, the Liverpool of the slave trade and the Irish famine emigration (the source of Heathcliff's origin, which the novel leaves unexplained), and the Calvinist and Methodist religious culture of the Brontë household.",
   sig="Wuthering Heights is the most radical novel of the Victorian period and the only one that has resisted every attempt to place it in a history of the novel's development: its double narration, its two-generation structure and its unassimilable passion made it a permanent test case for criticism (from Charlotte Brontë's 1850 preface to Kermode, Miller, Gilbert and Gubar, and the postcolonial reading of Heathcliff's racial origin). Its influence runs through the modern Gothic, the novel of the moors, and the fiction of the American South.",
   chars="Heathcliff; Catherine Earnshaw; Catherine Linton; Edgar Linton; Isabella Linton; Hindley Earnshaw; Hareton Earnshaw; Cathy Linton; Linton Heathcliff; Nelly Dean; Joseph; Mr Lockwood; Mr Earnshaw; Zillah",
   canon="Major canon",
   struct="Two framed narratives (Lockwood's outer frame and Nelly Dean's inner chronicle) covering two generations over some thirty years, with a symmetrical doubling of the first and second pairs of lovers."),
 dict(title="Gondal Poems", year="1846",
   gb="Pastoral Elegy and the Mourning Sequence, Sonnet Revival and Sequenced Sonnet", tb="Art versus life, and the romance of the artist, Bereavement, immortality, and the argument from grief",
   mb="Victorian High Seriousness and the Public Poet", comp=["1836", "1848"],
   form="Poems of a private imaginary chronicle",
   desc="The body of verse written by Emily (and, in part, Anne) Brontë from about 1836 into the 1840s, set in the imaginary island kingdom of Gondal in the North Pacific and concerned with its queens, its prisoners and its wars; a substantial part of it was printed in the 1846 volume, and the prose chronicles that framed it were lost.",
   plot="The poems are dramatic fragments rather than a continuous narrative: 'A. G. Rochelle' is a prisoner's song of endurance, 'The Death of A. G. Rochelle' and the Queen Augusta poems dramatise the politics of Gondal, and the elegiac and love poems register the passions of its royal houses. The recovering of the Gondal story from the poems has been a long scholarly exercise.",
   context="A childhood and adolescent imaginative project shared with Anne Brontë from 1827 onwards, descended from the Brontës' reading of Scott, Byron and Blackwood's, and conducted in manuscript notebooks (the 'Gondal poems' are preserved in the Ashley and Bonnell collections).",
   sig="The Gondal poems are the private laboratory of Emily Brontë's art and a central document for the study of the imaginative life of the Brontë household; they are the clearest English example of a nineteenth-century writer maintaining a complete secondary world across two decades.",
   alt=["The Gondal Poems"],
   canon="Secondary canon",
   attrib="The attribution of individual Gondal poems between Emily and Anne Brontë rests on manuscript evidence and on Charlotte's 1850 editing; a number remain uncertain, and the prose chronicles are lost.",
   status="partial"),
])

