   form="Three linked tales (Blackwood's Magazine, 1857); two volumes 1858",
   desc="Three tales — 'The Sad Fortunes of the Rev. Amos Barton', 'Mr Gilfil's Love-Story' and 'Janet's Repentance' — published in Blackwood's Edinburgh Magazine in 1857 and collected in 1858: the first fiction of George Eliot, written when she was already the author of translations and the editor of the Westminster Review.",
   plot="'Amos Barton' follows the failure and death of a dull curate's wife in the parish of Shepperton; 'Mr Gilfil's Love-Story' recovers the tragic past of the old vicar of Knebley; 'Janet's Repentance' traces the conversion of a drunken wife-beating clergyman's wife in the town of Milby through the ministry of the Evangelical Mr Tryan.",
   context="The Warwickshire and Staffordshire of the 1820s-30s, the Evangelical revival inside the Church of England, and the provincial town of the pre-Reform era; the stories were modelled on the recollections of Eliot's aunt, Elizabeth Tomlinson.",
   sig="Scenes of Clerical Life introduced the fictional method that Eliot developed for the next twenty years — the provincial community studied through several ordinary lives, the sympathy that refuses moral simplification, and the narrator who speaks as a member of the community — and it established her as the most serious new novelist of the 1850s.",
