   form="Novel in three volumes",
   desc="Published in 1866, set in the Midlands at the time of the first Reform Bill (1832), and the novel in which Eliot confronted electoral politics most directly: a study of radicalism, inheritance and class loyalty in the town of Treby Magna.",
   plot="Felix Holt, a watchmaker and Radical, and Harold Transome, the heir of Transome Court who returns from the East to stand as a Radical, are rivals for the hand of Esther Lyon, who is revealed to be the true heir of the Transome estate; the election riot, the trial of Felix for manslaughter, and the family secret of the Transome inheritance resolve into Esther's renunciation of her claim and her marriage to Felix.",
   context="The General Election of 1832 and the Reform Act, the decline of the landed estate, the money of the East India trade, and the religious Dissent of the provincial town.",
   sig="Felix Holt is the most direct Victorian novel about parliamentary reform and the most serious attempt by a major novelist to represent working-class political conviction; its analysis of the transference of estates and of the rhetoric of the hustings is a valuable document for the social history of 1832, and its heroine's choice is one of Eliot's most carefully argued moral conclusions.",
